---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/apps/sendlane';
const title = 'Sendlane for Shopify: preserve service and account continuity';
const desc = 'Review Sendlane after the Privy acquisition with account continuity, service scope, migration acceptance and lifecycle ownership exercises.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a class="link" href="/apps">← All app reviews</a>
    <p class="badge mt-6">Shopify operations · checked November 25, 2026</p>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Sendlane for Shopify: preserve service and account continuity</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9">Sendlane pairs ecommerce email and SMS with a service-oriented approach. For an existing Shopify operator, the first decision is how the supported account, people and active journeys continue after the Privy acquisition. For a prospective buyer, establish the offered product and service scope before comparing a legacy Sendlane plan with another sender. This is a workflow review, with proposed exercises rather than observed merchant results.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>What the acquisition establishes</h2>
      <p><a class="link" href="https://www.privy.com/blog/privy-acquires-sendlane">Privy announced its acquisition of Sendlane in February 2026</a>. <a class="link" href="https://www.sendlane.com/blog/privy-acquires-sendlane">Sendlane’s customer announcement</a> says existing customers can continue to expect hands-on support and expertise, with investment in email, SMS, automation and connected insights. That is useful continuity evidence; it does not specify an automatic account transfer, a shutdown date, a replacement contract or entitlement parity.</p>
      <p>Ask support to identify the current login, billing entity, renewal terms and named escalation route for your account. Confirm which Shopify connection and interface are supported for new installations, reconnections and existing operations. Keep written answers with the account inventory; acquisition branding alone cannot tell an operator which credentials or queues to change.</p>
      <p>The positive reason to consider this approach is access to people who can help translate a retention plan into operating workflows. Specify what that help includes: event mapping, template production, deliverability investigation, approvals, training and ongoing changes. Treat these as proposal questions, not a promise of a fully managed program or a fixed launch date.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Continuity inventory before changing a sender</h2>
      <p>Inventory authenticated domains, reply addresses, SMS sender identities, Shopify credentials, segments, templates and active flows. For each cart, welcome or post-purchase journey, record its entry event, eligibility filter, delay, discount rule, pending jobs and operator. Save accessible historical reports and permission evidence before any planned transition.</p>
      <p>Ask whether identifiers, historical events, unsubscribe records and attribution history remain available in the supported account. Match a sample of known customer and order IDs across exports and the destination. Distinguish missing data from a changed label or reporting window; do not recreate suppressed people as fresh subscribers.</p>
      <p>Agree on an escalation exercise: report a missing Shopify purchase event, identify who investigates the connector versus the message logic, and document the response and pause procedure. A support promise becomes reviewable through a named owner, deliverable and deadline. Neither a sales demonstration nor a successful test message proves production continuity.</p>
      <p>Keep the previous sender disabled but recoverable where the contract permits. Assign one sender ownership of each trigger, cancel predecessor jobs and prevent historical event replays from issuing fresh welcome offers. Agree who can authorize rollback and how to avoid reactivating old queues during it.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Four proposed lifecycle acceptance exercises</h2>
      <p>These exercises retain the cart, subscription, browse and winback jobs that make ecommerce messaging useful. Verify the required controls in the actual supported account; none has been run here.</p>
      <p><strong>Supplement cart migration:</strong> rebuild one cart journey from a documented event mapping with sending disabled. Use a returning customer, a corrected email address and a checkout completed during the delay. Check product links and order identity, replay the same event and withdraw permission before dispatch. Acceptance requires the intended single queue entry, a canceled obsolete offer and an operator-visible reason for suppression.</p>
      <p><strong>Subscription plus one-time purchase:</strong> separate essential usage information from an optional cross-sell. Record which source provides subscription state and how a cancellation, refund or delayed renewal reaches the account. Try an order containing both item types. Confirm the proposed upsell excludes an existing subscriber when inappropriate and does not treat service-message eligibility as marketing permission.</p>
      <p><strong>Denim browse:</strong> propose a collection-specific email only for identifiable, eligible visitors. Compare a known shopper, shared-device visit and anonymous browse; do not infer identity from a pageview alone. Verify that a changed product or out-of-stock variant cannot create a broken recommendation. Compare content variants using equivalent eligible groups, rather than claiming superiority from unrelated vendor examples.</p>
      <p><strong>Home-goods winback:</strong> define lapse from purchase cadence, then compare useful email content with an optional SMS step among separately eligible contacts. An unopened email is not authorization for SMS. Check a recent purchase arriving during the wait and an opt-out between channels. Coordinate offer caps and reply ownership, then measure contribution and complaints across a defined observation window.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Compare the same workload and the same human work</h2>
      <p>Use a hypothetical 14,000-contact month with 38,000 emails and 2,000 SMS as a quote specification. Ask how contacts, sends, message segments, destinations, carrier charges, sender numbers and overages are billed. Include onboarding, parallel operation, creative labor and exit costs. Do not reuse legacy entry prices as a current offer.</p>
      <p>For a 22,000-profile supplement migration, count the flows and data fields that must be rebuilt and who signs each off. A lower software quote does not establish lower operating cost if your team inherits more implementation work. Conversely, included assistance has value only when its deliverables cover the work you would otherwise pay for.</p>
      <p>Illustrative contribution math: 40 incremental orders at $18 contribution each produce $720 before program costs. With $500 of incremental messaging and labor, that leaves $220. The inputs are hypothetical; attributed orders are not automatically incremental orders. Use randomized eligible assignment and a suitable holdout where feasible, accounting for refunds, discounts, sample size and uncertainty.</p>
      <p>Compare support proposals alongside software: required approvals, revisions, urgent pauses, documentation and export access. Separate one-time migration costs from recurring service. No order-volume threshold establishes affordability, and no assumed two-month payback should decide the contract.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Choose continuity, consolidation or a new workflow deliberately</h2>
      <p>The actual <a class="link" href="/apps/privy">Privy review</a> follows onsite offers, usable signup economics and capture-to-message handoffs. This Sendlane review follows the service relationship and preservation of existing accounts and journeys. Read both when acquisition communication affects your store, without assuming that every Privy capture feature is enabled for every legacy Sendlane account.</p>
      <p>The <a class="link" href="/apps/shopify-email">Shopify Email / Messaging review</a> instead starts from native customer data, Flow and channel eligibility. Use that native acceptance checklist as a competing implementation proposal: can your team deliver the required journeys itself, and what outside assistance would still be needed? Existing <a class="link" href="/apps/omnisend">Omnisend</a> and <a class="link" href="/apps/klaviyo">Klaviyo</a> reviews are further reading, not fresh validation of their prices or comparative superiority.</p>
      <p>After any handover, keep a weekly queue and exception review: stale carts, duplicate entries, missing purchases, unsubscribes, reply backlog and unexplained report changes. Require an editable runbook with trigger mappings and pause instructions before onboarding ends. Preserve current channel and purpose-specific permission, and validate the suppression path at dispatch.</p>
      <p>Continue the supported account when it meets these requirements. Consolidate or replace it when a concrete unsupported workflow, service gap or matched cost comparison justifies the change. A compulsory second email sender, fixed revenue cutoff or platform rank does not resolve those decisions.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
